      ],
      "topics": [
        "arrays-strings-js",
        "hashmaps-objects-js",
        "two-pointers-js",
        "recursion-basics-js",
        "sorting-js"
      ],
      "intro": "Frontend fresher interviews almost always include a 30-minute coding round in JavaScript \u2014 not an algorithmic deep dive, but array manipulation, string transformation, and a hash map problem. This module covers arrays and strings with JS-specific methods (.map, .filter, .reduce, .split, .join), using plain objects and the Map type for frequency counts, the two-pointer and sliding-window patterns applied to string/array problems, basic recursion (factorial, fibonacci, flatten a nested array), and the sort comparison function (why [1,10,2].sort() gives the wrong answer and how to fix it). Everything is in vanilla JS, no imports \u2014 exactly what a HackerRank or CoderPad fresher screen looks like."
    },
    {
      "moduleNumber": "M13",
      "pillar": "P04",
      "pillarName": "React Fundamentals",
      "moduleSlug": "state-management-basics",
      "title": "State Management Basics",
      "appUrl": "/frontend-fresher/state-management-basics",
      "seoSlug": "react-state-management-interview-questions-freshers",
      "seoUrl": "/react-state-management-interview-questions-freshers",
      "altSlugs": [
        "react-context-interview-questions-freshers"
      ],
      "altUrls": [
        "/react-context-interview-questions-freshers"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "what-is-state-management",
        "lifting-state-up",
        "context-api-basics",
        "usereducer-pattern",
        "zustand-intro"
      ],
      "intro": "State management is one of the most-asked React topics even at the fresher level \u2014 \"how do you share state between components?\" is a universal interview question. This module explains why you need state management in the first place (prop drilling becomes unmanageable), how to solve it with Context API (createContext, Provider, useContext), when useReducer is cleaner than useState for multi-field state updates, and a brief intro to Zustand as a minimal external store. The goal is not to master Redux \u2014 it is to answer state management questions coherently and show you understand the problem, not just the API."
    },
